  {
    "id": 35,
    "name": "Spaanse Kade",
    "lat": 51.919944,
    "lng": 4.492083,
    "city": "Rotterdam",
    "architect": "Piet Blom",
    "era": "1981-1990",
    "social_org": "social",
    "scale": ">201",
    "note": null,
    "bag_id": null,
    "description": "The Spanish Quay is one of four projects within the larger redevelopment of Rotterdam’s Old Harbour, alongside the Potloodgebouw, Cube Houses and Zuidflat, all designed by Piet Blom. The project consists largely of social housing and is deliberately positioned along the waterfront, making harbour views accessible to lower-income residents.\n\nWhile Blom’s earlier concept of the “urban roof” could not be directly applied due to the required high housing density, the Spanish Quay reflects the same social ambition. The housing is placed on an elevated platform that forms a collective ground, with small squares and pedestrian routes intended to stimulate encounter and everyday interaction. Together with cafés and small businesses along the quay, the project creates a village-like environment within the city, reminiscent of a small Mediterranean harbour town.\n\nThe development accommodates a wide variety of dwelling types, yet larger units are not more luxurious but simply contain more rooms. In this way, Blom’s strongly anti-elitist vision becomes spatially visible, expressing his search for a more classless form of urban living."
  },
